    "round": 2,
    "role": "maker",
    "content": "Done — you have a working bookmark manager running locally. 🔖\n\n**Open it now:** http://localhost:5173 (the dev server is running in the background)\n\n### What I built\nA **local-first web app** — React + TypeScript frontend, Express + SQLite backend. Your bookmarks persist to `data/bookmarks.db` on your machine.\n\n**Features working end-to-end (I smoke-tested each):**\n- ✅ Add / edit / delete bookmarks (URL, title, notes)\n- ✅ Tags — click any tag to filter; live counts in the sidebar\n- ✅ Search across title, URL, and notes (debounced)\n- ✅ **\"Get title\"** button auto-fetches a page's `<title>` (verified against a live site)\n- ✅ Favicons, dark responsive UI\n- ✅ Server-side URL validation, orphan-tag cleanup, tag normalization (lowercase + dedupe)\n\n### Layout\n```\nserver/   index.ts · db.ts · bookmarks.ts · fetchTitle.ts   (API + SQLite)\nsrc/      App.tsx · api.ts · components/                     (React UI)\n```\n\n### Running it yourself later\n```bash\nnpm run dev      # starts both servers with hot reload\n```\nFrontend on `:5173`, API on `:3001` (proxied under `/api`). Full details and the API reference are in `README.md`.\n\n### A note\n- It's **single-user / no auth** by design (local-first). If you ever want to host it for multiple people, that's the main thing to add.\n- The only outbound network call is the optional \"Get title\" fetch — everything else stays local.\n\nWant me to add any of these next: **import/export** (pull in your existing browser bookmarks), a **browser extension** to save the current tab, or **accounts** so it can be hosted? I left these as \"next steps\" in the README too.",
